          "text": "other eukaryotes,\nhave organelles called mitochondria that supply the cell with chemical energy.\n\nMitochondria are organelles that\nexpress their own DNA and are believed to be the remnants of a symbiotic relationship between a eukaryote and an\nengulfed prokaryote. The mitochondrial DNA of trypanosomes exhibit an interesting exception to the central dogma:\ntheir pre-mRNAs do not have the correct information to specify a functional protein. Usually, this is because the\nmRNA is missing several U nucleotides. The cell performs an additional RNA processing step called RNA editing to\nremedy this. 15.4 • RNA Processing in Eukaryotes\n403",
