          "text": "elongation and termination.\n\nAlthough the enzymatic process of elongation is essentially the same in eukaryotes and prokaryotes, the DNA\ntemplate is considerably more complex. When eukaryotic cells are not dividing, their genes exist as a diffuse mass\nof DNA and proteins called chromatin. The DNA is tightly packaged around charged histone proteins at repeated\nintervals. These DNA–histone complexes, collectively called nucleosomes, are regularly spaced and include 146\nnucleotides of DNA wound around eight histones like thread around a spool. For polynucleotide synthesis to occur, the transcription machinery needs to move histones out of the way every time\nit encounters a nucleosome.",
